{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"humor-essay-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Humour essay track","description":"Takes a funny personal essay or humour column from a true story to a polished draft in gated steps, from premise and material through structure and draft to a punch-up and cut pass.","category":"humor","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build","review"],"role":["writer","content-creator","individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["notes","text"],"output":["outline","article","rewrite"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["humour-writing","personal-essay","humour-column","comic-nonfiction","self-editing"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-personal-essay","fix-joke-that-fell-flat","punch-up-with-humor"],"personas":["comedy-writing-partner"]},"args":[{"name":"story_notes","description":"The true story or situation, in rough notes, with the moments you remember, how you felt then and now, and the people involved.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"outlet_and_length","description":"Where it will run and the target length, for example \"my newsletter, 1,000 words\", \"magazine back page, 700 words\".","type":"string","default":"blog post, 1,000 words"}],"steps":[{"id":"premise","file":"steps/01-premise.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"material","file":"steps/02-material.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"structure","file":"steps/03-structure.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"draft","file":"steps/04-draft.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"punch-up","file":"steps/05-punch-up.md","stage":"review","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Turns a true story into a funny personal essay or humour column for {{outlet_and_length}}, one approved step at a time: the comic premise, the material, the structure, the draft, then a punch-up and cut pass. Humour essays work when the writer is the main target, the jokes serve a real feeling underneath, and every detail is true. Each step stops for the writer's approval or edits, and later steps build on the approved versions. If the writer asks to skip the approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining steps in one reply and state each choice made at a skipped gate. Never invent events, quotes or people: anything not in the notes is marked as a question for the writer.","steps":[{"id":"premise","file":"steps/01-premise.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Premise\n\nFind what the essay is really about and why it is funny.\n\nStory notes:\n{{story_notes}}\nOutlet and length: {{outlet_and_length}}\n\n1. If the notes do not contain a specific event or situation, ask for one in a single message and stop.\n2. Offer three possible premises, each in one sentence: the comic gap (between what the writer expected and what happened, or between how they see themselves and how they behaved) and the true feeling underneath (embarrassment, love, fear of ageing, wanting to belong).\n3. For each, name the comic attitude (bewildered, defensive, mock-heroic, deadpan) and who the main target is. The writer should be the main target; anyone else in the story gets gentle treatment.\n4. Recommend one premise and say why it fits the outlet.\n\nStop and wait for the writer to pick or adjust the premise."},{"id":"material","file":"steps/02-material.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Material\n\nGather everything the essay can use for the approved premise.\n\n1. List the true moments from the notes that serve the premise, each with the specific detail that makes it vivid (an object, a line someone said, a number).\n2. Mark the strongest moment as the centrepiece scene.\n3. List comic devices the material supports: running gags and callbacks, lists, understatement, exaggeration of the writer's own reaction, mock-formal language.\n4. Ask up to five questions for missing details that would make scenes funnier (what exactly was said, what the writer was wearing, what they did next).\n5. Flag anything that could embarrass or hurt someone else in the story, or reveal private information, and suggest leaving it out or checking with them.\n\nStop and wait for the writer to answer the questions and approve the material."},{"id":"structure","file":"steps/03-structure.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Structure\n\nShape the approved material into an outline sized to the target length.\n\n1. Write an opening line option set (three options) that starts in the middle of the situation or with a confession, not with background.\n2. Outline the essay in beats with approximate word counts: opening scene, the setup of the writer's expectation, escalation through two or three moments, the centrepiece scene, the turn where the true feeling shows, and an ending that calls back to the opening.\n3. Place each running gag and its callback in the outline.\n4. Mark where the tone turns sincere and keep that section free of jokes.\n\nStop and wait for the writer to approve the outline and an opening line."},{"id":"draft","file":"steps/04-draft.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Draft\n\nWrite the full draft from the approved outline.\n\n1. Write the essay at the target length in the writer's voice, first person, past or present tense as fits the material.\n2. Keep jokes short and end sentences on the funny word; let scenes run on detail rather than explaining the joke.\n3. Use only true events and details from the notes and the writer's answers; put [CHECK: …] where you had to assume something.\n4. Give the word count and a one-line list of places you were least sure were funny.\n\nStop and wait for the writer's notes on the draft."},{"id":"punch-up","file":"steps/05-punch-up.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Punch-up and cut\n\nTighten the approved draft for publication.\n\n1. Cut: remove explanation after punchlines, repeated jokes, throat-clearing openings and any paragraph the essay works without; aim for 10 to 15 percent shorter unless already at length.\n2. Punch up: for the five weakest jokes, give a stronger alternative each, marked so the writer can choose.\n3. Check the callbacks land and the sincere turn is not undercut by a joke straight after it.\n4. Check fairness: everyone else in the story would recognise themselves and not feel mocked; private details are gone.\n5. Deliver the final draft, the word count, three headline options and the list of remaining [CHECK] items."}]}
